From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 02 Nov 2026 06:21:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/rockchip: vop2: Refuse a DCLK outside the pixel clock tolerance

When neither the CRU nor the video PLL can make a DCLK exactly, VOP2
refuses the state for HDMI but lets every other output run at the
closest rate the CRU offers, however far off it is. On an Orange Pi 5
with HDMI holding V0PLL at 593.407 MHz, a 241.5 MHz DisplayPort mode
runs at 237.6 MHz (GPLL / 5, 1.6 % slow, measured), and before the VOP
DCLK dividers rounded to the closest rate 3840x2160@29.97Hz ran at
262.144 MHz (AUPLL / 3, 11.6 % slow). The DP sink is told the mode
clock and gets another one.

Sinks accept a pixel clock within 0.5 % of the mode's: the CTA-861 and
VESA DMT timings allow that much deviation (DRM's own mode matching uses
a fixed 5 kHz, which is no help here). Accept the closest
CRU rate for the other outputs only within that tolerance, and refuse
the state with -EINVAL otherwise, like a mode the hardware cannot
display, instead of committing it at a wrong rate.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/rockchip_drm_vop2.c | 25 +++++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/rockchip_drm_vop2.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/rockchip_drm_vop2.c
index d8541f0a1cfe..2b54e9ecb851 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/rockchip_drm_vop2.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/rockchip/rockchip_drm_vop2.c
@@ -2538,6 +2538,15 @@ static bool vop2_dclk_rate_exact(long rate, unsigned long clock)
 	return rate > 0 && abs_diff((unsigned long)rate, clock) < 1000;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Sinks accept a pixel clock within 0.5 % of the mode's (CTA-861, VESA DMT),
+ * the tolerance DRM itself uses to match modes.
+ */
+static bool vop2_dclk_rate_close(long rate, unsigned long clock)
+{
+	return rate > 0 && abs_diff((unsigned long)rate, clock) * 200 <= clock;
+}
+
 /*
  * The CRU parents of dclk_vopN_src (GPLL, CPLL, V0PLL, AUPLL) only give
  * integer fractions of their current rates. If none of them makes the DCLK
@@ -2602,10 +2611,11 @@ static unsigned long vop2_crtc_dclk_pll_claimed(struct vop2_video_port *vp,
  * the PHY supports, but when the DCLK has to come from the system CRU (FRL
  * mode, or rates beyond the PHY PLL), it is derived by integer division
  * from a few fixed PLLs, and the clock framework silently picks the closest
- * lower rate: e.g. 3840x2160@59.94Hz (593.407 MHz) would run at 500 MHz.
- * Use the video PLL for such a rate when it is available (for any output),
- * and refuse the HDMI configuration otherwise instead of scanning out at a
- * wrong rate.
+ * rate: e.g. 3840x2160@59.94Hz (593.407 MHz) would run at 594 MHz.
+ * Use the video PLL for such a rate when it is available (for any output).
+ * Otherwise refuse the configuration instead of scanning out at a wrong
+ * rate: HDMI unless the CRU rate is exact, the other outputs unless it is
+ * within the pixel clock tolerance.
  */
 static int vop2_crtc_atomic_check_dclk(struct vop2_video_port *vp,
 				       struct drm_crtc_state *crtc_state)
@@ -2692,9 +2702,12 @@ static int vop2_crtc_atomic_check_dclk(struct vop2_video_port *vp,
 		return 0;
 	}
 
-	/* Other outputs keep running at the closest rate, as before */
-	if (!hdmi)
+	if (!hdmi && vop2_dclk_rate_close(rate, clock)) {
+		drm_dbg_kms(vop2->drm,
+			    "vp%d: DCLK %lu Hz from the closest CRU rate %ld Hz\n",
+			    vp->id, clock, rate);
 		return 0;
+	}
 
 	drm_dbg_kms(vop2->drm,
 		    "vp%d: DCLK %lu Hz not available from the CRU (closest %ld Hz)\n",
-- 
2.55.0

